core_net_debt_ebitda":88.9,"_gate_accruals":0.05559661930554465,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":2.4,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.1067087691574522,"_gp_rev_volatility":true,"_score_rev_volatility":83.8,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.005100097338611095,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":93.6,"_gate_piotroski":2,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":22.2,"_gate_spread":0.09599870005544453,"_gp_spread":true,"_score_spread":48.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":0.30265037258673466,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":49.6,"_gate_incr_roic":0.11889923187977611,"_gp_incr_roic":true,"_score_incr_roic":56.3,"_gate_margin_advantage":0.04025514017566234,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":46.8,"_gate_pool_share":-0.054425602172895715,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":18.2,"_gate_rev_durability":0.12031641445670682,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":85.2,"_gate_fcf_durability":-0.03999074195919161,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":5.0,"_gate_margins":-0.0015744232651907996,"_gp_margins":false,"_score_margins":48.4,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.06293992682635441,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":81.0,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.029671140781786454,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":87.1,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.003847633008517172,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":93.6,"_gate_insider_own":0.02211,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":14.7,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"M/I Homes, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, engages in the construction and sale of single-family residential homes in Ohio, Indiana, Illinois, Minnesota, Michigan, Florida, Texas, North Carolina, and Tennessee. The company operates through Northern Homebuilding, Southern Homebuilding, and Financial Services segments. It also designs, constructs, markets, and sells single-family homes and attached townhomes to first-time, move-up, empty-nester, multi-generational, and luxury homebuyers under the M/I Homes brand name. In addition, the company purchases undeveloped land to develop into developed lots for the construction of single-family homes, as well as for sale to others. Further, it originates and sells mortgages; and serves as a title insurance agent by providing title insurance policies, examination, and closing services to purchasers of its homes. The company was formerly known as M/I Schottenstein Homes, Inc. and changed its name to M/I Homes, Inc. in January 2004. M/I Homes, Inc. was founded in 1976 and is based in Columbus, Ohio.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Robert H. Schottenstein J.D. | Chairman, President & CEO | Age 72 | Compensation: $5.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $2.5M ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, with revenue growing 16%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $71K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $9K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["M/I Homes, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $3.2B market cap on $4.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years and +12% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 20% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 10-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $128M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 3 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.34\u00d7, however, which raises the oldest question in analysis: are these earnings backed by cash?.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA.","Management returns a meaningful 6.3% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 2.2% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 97% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.9714200712476306},{"text":"Piotroski score 2/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $11.1M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Consumer Cyclical peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Consumer demand is fundamentally cyclical \u2014 a recession compresses both unit volume and pricing power, and operating leverage cuts both ways.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"E-commerce margin compression and tariff exposure \u2014 digital competition erodes pricing power while trade policy reshuffles supply-chain cost structures overnight.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Consumer Cyclical sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Is M/I Homes (M